WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.360
Joined by Derrick Williams from DW Science desk and Martin Gak from our Religion and Ethics department.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.400
Welcome to both of you. Let me start with you Derrick.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:15.400
Now first of all. How short can we be of this claim made by the scientists that he's genetically edited babies,

00:00:15.420 --> 00:00:19.420
because there's been no independent scientific verification so far has there?

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:22.300
No, there hasn't been there. This hasn't followed the normal past.

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.620
Generally, you would have had a publication with peer-review first of all, before it,

00:00:26.620 --> 00:00:33.400
but this researcher has made basically a claim online. And as of now, we don't have any real hard evidence to back it up.

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:38.440
Although he does have the background, and he does have the experience to have carried it out.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:43.140
But turning it to you, this scientist insists that what he's done with these babies

00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.580
is to protect them from a condition in this case HIV.

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.660
So he's actually says he's protecting them. What's wrong about that?

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.160
Well at first blush obviously nothing. I mean one would think this is quite commendable.

00:00:53.160 --> 00:00:58.540
But the fact is that this is a technology that is sort of at it's very incipient stage, and furthermore,

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:03.360
the problem is that there is absolutely no way to measure what the impact of

00:01:03.460 --> 00:01:06.640
any one, sort of, gene editing operation will be.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:10.160
I mean, bear in mind that you would be releasing these people into the population.

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:16.160
Provided that they grow up and, these two, these twins actually reproduce and have children of their own.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:20.420
They will actually be perpetrating this genetic format, I mean, bringing it into the genetic pool.

00:01:20.500 --> 00:01:26.300
Evidently, we don't know, if this has actually taken place or not, but to judge by the reaction of the international

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:32.800
scientific community, I think we can assume that everybody understands that this is a possibility.

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:37.700
A serious possibility that this scientist is capable of doing, it and I think he's correct,

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:41.360
in saying that if it's not him, it's imminent that this is gonna happen.

00:01:41.369 --> 00:01:45.299
So, a lot of the questions here, as far as I'm concerned, is about, once again regulation.

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:47.398
How are we gonna regulate this kind of technology?

00:01:47.399 --> 00:01:53.069
So you have some serious ethical concerns about this, but what about you Derrick as a scientist?

00:01:53.180 --> 00:01:54.100
Well, I agree completely.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.340
The technology is there, but it's been there actually for the last eight years,

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:00.860
and it's been clear that we could use this technology to do this.

00:02:00.860 --> 00:02:04.620
We haven't up until now. We've been doing mostly things with plants, with animals,

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:07.520
but the possibility has always been open.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.800
People have been, that's why it's been a taboo. Things only become taboo when they become possible,

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.980
and this is definitely become a possibility.

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:19.740
And so the question is now, I think, what we need to be moving towards,

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:23.880
both the scientific community and the world as a whole, is global regulation of this kind of technology.

00:02:23.880 --> 00:02:28.060
But are we really, it's not no more than at a national level. Where things are possibly allowed,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:31.140
as is the case in China within certain guidelines.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:36.300
Or they're at least not completely forbidden, whereas in other places, they might be.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.000
I think it's worth pointing out that...

00:02:39.140 --> 00:02:41.940
Nobody should think that this is something this big can be pulled back.

00:02:42.180 --> 00:02:45.340
I mean, if as a matter of fact this technology is there, and it's being used,

00:02:45.380 --> 00:02:49.840
there is no way that this will stop being used because we actually think it shouldn't.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:53.100
I mean, this is precisely why we need international regulations,

00:02:53.180 --> 00:02:55.780
because the technology is already there and it's usable.

00:02:55.980 --> 00:02:58.840
The thing about regulation is that often the boundaries

00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:02.640
between scientific discovery and ethical concerns are sometimes blurred.

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.470
So how does one regulate this kind of development?

00:03:05.470 --> 00:03:13.040
I think that this is becoming, it's not sometimes blurred, more and more often that technology and ethics

00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.120
are coming into conflict with one another. We need, and we obviously from from my point of view.

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:21.220
We need to to answer most of the ethical concerns,

00:03:21.220 --> 00:03:25.120
or many of the ethical concerns, before we begin to employ the technology in certain ways.

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:28.340
Unfortunately, it generally tends to happen in the other direction.

00:03:28.820 --> 00:03:30.320
Yeah, very clearly. I mean.

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:34.260
I think that what we actually find in the scientific discourse and the scientific tradition,

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.660
and the way that we implement technology, because bear in mind,

00:03:36.740 --> 00:03:41.460
that most people out there in the world will actually see science through technical developments.

00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:46.360
I mean some somebody will want to get pregnant, not be able, and then they will find a scientific development

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:51.600
in in vitro fertilization or something like that. This kind of things tend to happen in precisely a door there

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:56.920
It's like we engage in certain practices, and then we think okay, is this the best way?

00:03:56.980 --> 00:04:00.280
Or do we actually need to put different forms of, you know, different controls?

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:05.120
At this stage, I think that element here is that it has a certain macabre aspect

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:07.580
and there is something Frankensteinian about this story.

00:04:07.580 --> 00:04:10.780
Somebody had that has somehow concocted a human being.

00:04:10.840 --> 00:04:15.640
I think that that's this disingenuous, I mean, it just helps to actually blur the problem.

00:04:15.700 --> 00:04:18.460
Which is that as a matter of fact that we have a very powerful technology.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:21.100
We don't have a very clear understanding of where it could lead.

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:26.480
What it could mean, but it is something that for us could be actually an incredibly powerful tool.

00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:27.460
So, we have a responsibility.

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:31.100
Sorry, we have a responsibility to make good use of it so to say.

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:36.120
Derrick you yourself are a specialist in biology, and you know that this area of genetic science

00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:40.600
is very very competitive with scientists looking at ways to outdo each other.

00:04:40.840 --> 00:04:43.640
How do you see this when you see this? Where do you see this development going?

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.520
Because as a scientist said in our report, "If not me somebody else would have done it."

00:04:47.840 --> 00:04:50.560
I think that that's probably true.

00:04:50.620 --> 00:04:56.620
I also think, that you can't throw the baby out with the bathwater, if you'll excuse me in this particular instance,

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:04.460
CRISPR Cas9 technologies is an incredibly powerful tool for doing a wide, wide range of things.

00:05:04.560 --> 00:05:08.200
Including doing things like providing plants with drought resistance.

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:11.360
We're able to go in and tinker with evolution

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:15.180
and things that might have taken many many generations to happen in the past.

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:18.620
We can now make happen very very quickly and very very easily.

00:05:18.640 --> 00:05:22.540
But just because we can make them happen, doesn't mean that we should,

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:25.900
particularly when it involves the human race, and possibly the future of humanity.

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:30.820
Right. So the implications are huge, both on the ethical front as well as on the scientific front.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:35.900
Derek Williams from our Science desk, and Martin Gak our Religious and Ethics Affairs correspondent.

00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:37.840
Thank you very much for your perspectives.

00:05:37.920 --> 00:05:39.100
You're welcome.

